    "result_text": "The full build is green. Here's what I implemented to satisfy the spec (reject a supplied `registrationDate` later than the server date with a 400):\n\n**New escalation (checked exception + handler + YAML)**\n- `FutureRegistrationDateException` \u2014 carries the offending date.\n- `FutureRegistrationDateExceptionHandler` \u2014 responds 400 with an RFC-7807 `ProblemDetail` and an `errors: [\"registrationDate\"]` property, matching the shape of the other 400 handlers (`InvalidTelephoneException`, etc.).\n- `officefloor/escalation/...FutureRegistrationDateException.yml` \u2014 routes the exception to the handler.\n\n**New validation step**\n- `RegistrationDates.isFuture(LocalDate)` \u2014 a reusable rule alongside the existing `effective`/`toBusinessDay` helpers; a null (omitted) date is never \"in the future\".\n- `ValidateRegistrationDate` \u2014 reads the already-bound body via `@Val OwnerFieldsDto` and throws when the supplied date is after `LocalDate.now()`.\n\n**Pipeline wiring**\n- Inserted `validateRegistrationDate` into `owners.POST.yml` right after `validate` (which binds `@RequestBody` and publishes the DTO), so a future date is rejected early, before the load/write steps.\n\nA request that omits `registrationDate` is still accepted (it defaults to the server date in `BuildOwner`); only a supplied future date is rejected. `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0 \u2014 BUILD SUCCESS`.",
