    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change is a textbook fit for this codebase's conventions \u2014 each piece lands in the unit a maintainer would expect:\n\n- **`ValidateRegistrationDate`** mirrors the existing per-concern validation steps (`ValidateOwnerPostcode`): one public `service` method, reads the already-published `@Val OwnerFieldsDto request`, throws a single checked escalation. The project already decomposes create-time validation into one function per rule, so adding a dedicated step is additive and idiomatic rather than bloating `ValidateNewOwner` (which is scoped to required-fields + telephone/email).\n- **`RegistrationDates.isFuture`** belongs exactly where it was put \u2014 the class is explicitly the \"Shared rules for an owner's registration date,\" already owning `effective`/`toBusinessDay`/`countOn`. The null-safe, `LocalDate.now()`-based predicate is consistent with its siblings and keeps the date logic in one home rather than inlining `isAfter(...)` in the function.\n- **`FutureRegistrationDateException`** and its handler are faithful copies of the `InvalidTelephoneException`/`...Handler` pair: checked exception carrying the offending value, handler routing through the shared `ProblemDetails.build` helper and setting the `errors` property. No new response shape invented.\n- **Wiring** (escalation YAML + POST pipeline insertion after `validate`, before `validatePostcode`) follows the documented step/escalation layout, and placing it among the pre-build validations matches the comment's intent (reject before `BuildOwner` defaults/adjusts the date).\n\nNo duplication, nothing becoming a catch-all, logic sits in the right layer.",
